        original:
          'If its working were not disturbed by other economic forces or other features of the emulative process, the immediate effect of such a pecuniary struggle as has just been described in outline would be to make men industrious and frugal. This result actually follows, in some measure, so far as regards the lower classes, whose ordinary means of acquiring goods is productive labour. This is more especially true of the labouring classes in a sedentary community which is at an agricultural stage of industry, in which there is a consi­derable subdivision of property, and whose laws and customs secure to these classes a more or less definite share of the product of their industry. These lower classes can in any case not avoid labour, and the imputation of labour is therefore not greatly derogatory to them, at least not within their class. Rather, since labour is their recognised and accepted mode of life, they take some emulative pride in a reputation for efficiency in their work, this being often the only line of emulation that is open to them. For those for whom acquisition and emulation is possible only within the field of productive efficiency and thrift, the struggle for pecuniary reputability will in some measure work out in an increase of diligence and parsimony. But certain secondary features of the emulative process, yet to be spoken of, come in to very materially circumscribe and modify emulation in these directions among the pecuniarily inferior classes as well as among the superior class.',
        zh: '倘若其运作不受其他经济力量或竞赛过程中其他特征的干扰，方才勾勒的这种金钱角逐，其直接效果本该是使人勤勉而节俭。就下层阶级而言，由于他们获取财货的通常手段是生产性劳动，这一结果在一定程度上确实发生了。在处于农业产业阶段的定居社群中，倘若财产已有相当程度的细分，且法律与习俗保障劳动阶级能在自身勤劳的产物中分得一份多半确定的份额，情形就更是如此。这些下层阶级无论如何都无法免于劳动，因而蒙受劳动之名对他们并无多大贬损，至少在其本阶级内部是如此。相反，既然劳动是他们公认并接受的生活方式，他们反倒会在干活得力的名声中抱有某种争胜的自豪感，因为这往往是唯一向他们敞开的竞赛途径。对于那些只能在生产效率与节俭的范围内求取财物并展开竞赛的人来说，争取金钱体面的角逐将在一定程度上表现为勤奋与俭省的增长。然而，竞赛过程中若干尚待论及的次生特征介入进来，无论在金钱地位较低的各阶级中，还是在上层阶级中，都极为实质性地限制并改变了沿着这些方向展开的竞赛。',
